  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 222 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low near the Saskatchewan and \nManitoba border, with trough dropping southward through central\nNorth Dakota, while high is over the Midwest. Upper level \nanalysis places high over New Mexico with ridge stretching to \nthe north, but top of ridge is being busted down by a compact \nlow over southern Saskatchewan which is gradually meandering to \nthe east. Over our area, aforementioned surface trough \ncontinues to push through, right now passing generally over and\njust east of the Highway 83 corridor, with high humidity ahead \nand drier air behind.\n\nFor the rest of this afternoon into this evening, aforementioned\ntrough will continue to gradually meander east. Behind the \ntrough, dry air continues to push in resulting in low relative \nhumidity values. This is especially noted over southwest into \nparts of northwest North Dakota where, combined with a westerly \nbreeze, near critical wildland fire conditions have developed. \n\nTo the east of the trough, elevated moisture remains in place\nwith CAPE values around 3000 J/Kg, and 0-6 km shear values\naround 40 kts, plenty for rotating updrafts. The question is at\nwhat point as the trough pushes east will storms develop, given\nthe forcing is not the strongest and there is some capping to\ndeal with. Guidance has wavered some between the 5-7 PM CDT\nhours, but the later it goes the further east the initiation is\nexpected. With that said, once initiation does occur, expect any\nstorm to develop rapidly, with discrete development given deep\nlayer shear vectors remain perpendicular to the boundary. With \nthat all said, storms should then move quickly off to the east, \nwith quiet weather then setting in for the rest of the night. \n\nOn Tuesday, mainly quiet weather is expected as the ridge axis \nre-focuses over the Rockies, though the aforementioned compact \nlow/short wave will move through southern Manitoba which may \nlead to a few showers around the Turtle Mountains region. \nNorthwest winds will be on the increase as the surface low with \nthis system moves east through Manitoba into Ontario. Upper \nridge gradually meanders east resulting in quiet weather for us \nthrough Thursday before pattern becomes more unsettled, \nbringing some low to medium precipitation chances to end the \nweek into the weekend.",
